---

<script lang="ts">
  import { cities, figures } from './parking/cities';
  import SquaresFigure from './parking/SquaresFigure.svelte';
  import Board from './parking/Board.svelte';
  import TreesAreCool from './parking/TreesAreCool.svelte';

  // Capitals whose parking data OpenStreetMap under-maps, listed at the foot of
  // the method section. The prototype built this string at runtime (#methodDynamic);
  // here it is derived from the same rows the ranking reads.
  const flagged = cities.filter((c) => c.thin || c.sparse);
</script>

<!-- Live figures come from `figures` in ./parking/cities.ts:
       nCities        number of capitals (39)
       medShare       median % of municipal residents living in the core
       medGreen       median capital's median square: % of its land that is green
       medCarSh       ditto, % that is car infrastructure
       medZero        median capital: % of residents whose square has no green
       medGreenAfter / medCarAfter / medZeroAfter   the same three, converted
       carWins        capitals whose median square holds more car than green
       flipCount      how many of those the conversion turns around
     Eight figures below are RETIRED — the per-resident m² numbers no longer
     exist in the payload. They render as loud orange ⟨name?⟩ placeholders and
     the sentences around them need rewriting before publishing. -->

<article class="pp">
  <section class="intro">
    <p class="kicker">Trees vs cars in {figures.nCities} European capitals</p>
    <h1>What if we got rid of cars?</h1>
    <p class="stand">
      It's getting hot in here and not in the good way.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only accross 27 EU countries, more than 16,000 people died
      <a href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/europes-heat-waves-are-killing-thousands-and-wringing-its-soils-dry/">because of the
      heat stress</a> of the 2026 June heatwave. One in three of the
      deaths driven by cities' own heat islands
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(22)02585-5">would be prevented
      by raising tree coverage</a>. Yet Europe is short of the green that
      does the cooling. More than 8 in 10 Europeans living in cities
      <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-71523-8">don't have
      enough trees and parks around them</a>, and 1 in 5 has essentially none.
    </p>
    <p>
      While ground in cities is scarce and valuable, there is one type of
      infrastructure we do allocate a lot of space to: cars. So I
      measured every road, surface parking lot and park in
      {figures.nCities} European capitals' urban cores to
      quantify it. In the median capital, an inhabitant gets
      <strong><span class="figmiss">⟨medCar?⟩</span> m² of car infrastructure and only
      <span class="figmiss">⟨medUgs?⟩</span> m² of parks</strong>; in
      {figures.carWins} of {figures.nCities}
      capitals, cars get more ground than parks.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="squares">
    <h2>500 metres at a time</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Every core here is cut into 500&nbsp;m squares — a five-minute walk across —
      and each square is measured on its own: how much of its land is green you can
      walk into, how much is carriageway and surface parking. Squares that are more
      water than land, or that hang off the edge of the core, are dropped. A city's
      figure is then its median square, weighted by the people who live in it, so a
      forest on the rim cannot stand in for a park downtown.
    </p>
    <div class="legend">
      <span><i style="background:#0a6b40"></i>Urban green space</span>
      <span><i style="background:#7d4a9e"></i>Roads</span>
      <span><i style="background:#6d3f96"></i>Surface parking</span>
      <span><i style="background:#c3d9e8"></i>Water</span>
    </div>
    <SquaresFigure />
  </section>
  <section class="board">
    <div class="board-head">
      <h2>Parks vs parking</h2>
      <p class="note">
        Urban green = spaces the public can enter and use for relief from heat: parks, gardens, cemeteries, forests, woods and nature reserves, private and public.<br>
        The NOW / CONVERTED buttons switch the list and the map between the city as
        measured and the scenario: every surface parking lot plus 60% of the calm
        streets given back as green.<br>
        The HEAT button on the map lays each city's modelled summer heat island over its streets.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Board rows={cities} />
  </section>
  <section class="why">
    <h2>Why trees are cool</h2>
    <p>
      Any surface can bounce energy back to the sky, store it, heat the air with
      it, or spend it evaporating water. Asphalt cannot evaporate water, so it heats the air more than anything.
      That is how an urban heat island emerges.
    </p>
    <TreesAreCool />
    <p>
      Asphalt's own surface can swing between roughly 40°C and 60°C on a sunny
      day, and it keeps releasing that stored heat into the air well after
      sunset. This is why cities stay hotter than the surrounding countryside,
      and it's what makes them suffocating in a heatwave.
    </p>
    <p>
      Trees, on the other hand, <a href="https://theconversation.com/can-trees-really-cool-our-cities-down-44099">cool the city down</a>. Their shade prevents asphalt
      from absorbing the heat in the first place, and they use the energy they
      absorb to evaporate water. It's basically tree sweating. This way, trees roughly
      <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-71825-x">halve the
      urban heat island effect</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="why">
    <h2>It's not that crazy</h2>
    <p>
      So how much green is actually on the table? Run one scenario on the ground I
      measured: turn every surface parking lot plus 60% of the calm local streets
      into green space, and leave the arterials alone. This is arithmetic on the
      measured ground, not a plan. Across the
      {figures.nCities} capitals it hands back a median of
      <strong><span class="figmiss">⟨medGain?⟩</span> m² of green per resident</strong>,
      lifting the median from <span class="figmiss">⟨medUgs?⟩</span> to
      <span class="figmiss">⟨medUgsAfter?⟩</span> m² — past the median car allocation of
      <span class="figmiss">⟨medCar?⟩</span> m², so the typical capital itself crosses
      from losing ground to cars to beating them. Of the
      {figures.carWins} capitals where cars currently out-measure
      parks, <strong>{figures.flipCount}</strong> would flip. The
      biggest single gain is Reykjavík's 29 m² a head; the smallest is Paris's
      1.8, because Paris has almost no surface parking left to reclaim.
    </p>
    <p>
      That last point is not hypothetical. When cities close streets or pull out
      parking, a chunk of the traffic doesn't reroute, it disappears, a phenomenon
      known as
      <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/248177303_Traffic_Evaporation_-_What_Really_Happens_When_Road_Space_Is_Reallocated">"traffic
      evaporation"</a>. Pontevedra, in western Spain,
      <a href="https://www.politico.eu/article/pontevedra-city-pioneer-europe-car-free-future/">banned
      most cars</a> from its center in 1999, the longest-running proof we have:
      traffic fell 53%, air pollution 67%, and the last traffic death was 15 years
      ago. Oslo
      <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/90294948/what-happened-when-oslo-decided-to-make-its-downtown-basically-car-free">cleared
      cars</a> from its downtown core and turned the parking into benches, bike
      lanes and pocket parks; in my numbers Oslo is one of the
      {figures.flipCount} that flip, from 11.9 to 21.8 m² of green,
      just past its 20.2 of car. Paris is removing 70,000 on-street parking spots
      and <a href="https://www.leparisien.fr/info-paris-ile-de-france-oise/transports/stationnement-a-paris-24-000-places-supprimees-en-six-ans-loin-des-70-000-promises-07-02-2026-ET2CCP54MBE2HE6343TMW2YG3E.php">24,000
      were gone</a> by early 2026 — which is why it already runs the leanest car
      footprint of all {figures.nCities}, 5.9 m² a head, and already
      keeps more green than car space.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of this needs the cars gone tomorrow. But the asphalt they sit on does
      nothing for a city in a heatwave, and the measurements say what the streets
      already show: give a median European capital its parking and a share of its
      quiet streets back as green, and it goes from
      <span class="figmiss">⟨medUgs?⟩</span> m² a head to
      <span class="figmiss">⟨medUgsAfter?⟩</span>, from losing to cars to beating them. So
      — what if we got rid of cars?
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="method">
    <h2>How this was measured</h2>
    <div class="mgrid">
      <div>
        <h3>Green, cars, people</h3>
        <p>
          Green is counted in <strong>two tiers</strong>. The headline, urban green
          space, is what a resident can actually walk into: OpenStreetMap polygons
          of parks, gardens, recreation grounds, commons, village greens, dog parks
          and cemeteries — the same public recreational categories the EU's
          <a href="https://land.copernicus.eu/en/products/urban-atlas">Urban
          Atlas</a> calls "green urban areas", and the same OSM tags used to
          monitor <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2073-445X/13/12/2174">UN SDG
          11.7.1</a> — plus nature reserves, and forest and wood together, since
          which of the two tags a mapper reaches for is a national convention, not
          a real distinction. Dissolved so overlaps count once, open water
          subtracted, and nothing under 0.5&nbsp;ha — the size at which the
          <a href="https://www.who.int/europe/publications/i/item/WHO-EURO-2016-3352-43111-60341">WHO's
          evidence review</a> starts counting a green space at all.
        </p>
        <p class="pad">
          What the headline still refuses: verge grass (no one's park), meadow,
          grassland, heath, scrub and gated allotments. The second tier,
          <strong>all green</strong>, adds every excluded category back in — kept
          in the published data — so the exclusion is auditable, not hidden. The
          <a href="https://www.eea.europa.eu/en/analysis/publications/who-benefits-from-nature-in-cities-social-inequalities-in-access-to-urban-green-and-blue-spaces-across-europe">EEA
          reports a similar split</a>: total green infrastructure averages 42% of a
          European city's area, publicly accessible green space 3%.
        </p>
        <p class="pad">
          Car infrastructure is every carriageway plus every surface parking area,
          dissolved together so overlaps count once. Roads are buffered from OSM
          centrelines: the tagged <code>width</code> where present, otherwise
          lanes × 3.25 m, otherwise
          <a href="https://www.cogitatiopress.com/urbanplanning/article/view/1209">defaults
          per road class</a>. Kerb-to-kerb means sidewalks and separated cycle tracks are
          excluded by construction; painted bike and bus lanes are included — a small
          car-favouring simplification. Where cities publish their own carriageway
          inventories the method lands within a few percent (Madrid 100%, Brussels
          99%, Stockholm 97%); Paris's exceptional street geometry needed a width
          calibration against the city's official road plan. Multi-storey and
          underground parking is excluded: this measures ground.
        </p>
        <p class="pad">
          The three headline figures live on a <strong>500&nbsp;m grid</strong>
          laid across each urban core — squares that are more water than land, or
          that hang off the core's edge, are dropped. Every square left gets its
          own green share and car share of its land, and its resident count from
          GHS-POP; both medians are population-weighted, squares sorted by value
          until cumulative population crosses half. "No green" is the share of a
          core's residents standing in a square with no green in it at all. The
          converted state re-runs all three with every surface parking lot and 60%
          of the calm-street area moved from car to green, arterials untouched —
          and it skips the 0.5&nbsp;ha floor the headline applies elsewhere: the
          median surface lot is 469&nbsp;m², 96.6% of lots fall under that
          threshold, and enforcing it would leave only about a third of the
          converted area standing — survival from 12% (Tirana) to 64% (Madrid).
        </p>
        <p class="pad">
          Residents are the EU JRC's GHS-POP 2025 grid summed inside the identical
          urban-core polygon that the green and the asphalt are measured in — the
          numerator and denominator can never refer to different places. Land
          areas exclude water via GHS-LAND, so Helsinki is judged on its land, not
          its sea. Water itself comes from OSM water polygons plus the
          OSM-coastline ocean product, and is subtracted from every green area:
          a bay inside a nature reserve is not green space.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Why the urban core, and honestly</h3>
        <p>
          Everything here is measured inside each capital's <strong>urban
          centre</strong> — the EU/UN Degree of Urbanisation definition (GHS-SMOD):
          the contiguous dense settlement, intersected with the municipal boundary —
          and divided by the residents of that same area. Administrative city limits
          are a trap this page refuses: Belgrade's "city" is a 3,200 km² district,
          Madrid's includes two regional nature parks, Zagreb's a mountain. Under
          each city's name we state what share of the municipal population lives in
          the core being measured; in compact capitals it is nearly everyone, in
          sprawling ones (Vilnius, Belgrade) a quarter to a third live outside it —
          their streets and green are excluded together with them, numerator and
          denominator alike.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What could be wrong</h3>
        <p>
          OpenStreetMap's road network is close to complete everywhere in Europe, so
          the road figures travel well. Parking completeness still varies by country:
          cities marked † have thin parking data, and every parking figure is a
          floor, not a ceiling. Kerbside mapping conventions differ the most.
        </p>
        <p class="pad">
          GHS-POP is a modelled 2025 estimate anchored to censuses, not a census:
          at municipal level it runs within ~5% of registers in most capitals and
          about −12% in Bratislava, whose unregistered residents no source counts
          well; core-level populations inherit those biases. Valletta is so small
          that its per-person figures carry real grid noise. The urban-centre
          outline itself is a 1 km grid, so the core's edge is blocky — features
          are clipped to it exactly, but a park straddling the edge counts only
          in part.
        </p>
        <p class="pad">
          And the deliberate asymmetry, now running the other way: the headline
          green is strict — no verge grass, no meadow, no allotments, no patch
          under 0.5&nbsp;ha — while car space is merely conservative (carriageway
          only, no sidewalks, parking under-mapped in several countries). Both
          exclusion lists are disclosed: everything cut from the green headline is
          still measured and kept in the published data.
          If anything, the green side is now the floor too — a page that
          once leaned green now leans honest.
        </p>
        {#if flagged.length}
          <p class="pad">Cities with thin parking data (†):
            {#each flagged as c, i}<strong>{c.city}</strong>{i < flagged.length - 1 ? ', ' : '.'}{/each}
          </p>
        {/if}
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="credit">
      Geometry © OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL (incl. ocean polygons via
      osmdata.openstreetmap.de). Population, land &amp; urban centres: EU JRC GHSL
      (GHS-POP R2023A, GHS-LAND R2022A, GHS-SMOD R2023A).
      Urban heat island: Copernicus C3S / VITO UrbClim intensity model via the
      EEA, CC BY 4.0.
      Paris road widths calibrated against Ville de Paris open data (Plan de
      Voirie). Analysis and code by Ada Homolová.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>

